      "text": "To determine the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) such that \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of \\( 3 \\) and \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we start by letting \\( x = 3k \\), where \\( k \\) is an integer. Then we need to find the maximum \\( k \\) such that \\( (3k)^3 < 1000 \\). This simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n27k^3 < 1000\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\( k^3 \\), we divide both sides of the inequality by 27:\n\n\\[\nk^3 < \\frac{1000}{27}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the value of \\( \\frac{1000}{27} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1000}{27} \\approx 37.037\n\\]\n\nThe greatest integer \\( k \\) that satisfies \\( k^3 < 37.037 \\) is \\( k = 3 \\). We verify this by calculating \\( 3^3 \\):\n\n\\[\n3^3 = 27\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 27 < 1000 \\), \\( k = 3 \\) is indeed a valid solution. To confirm that \\( k = 3 \\) is the greatest possible value, we check the next integer, \\( k = 4 \\):\n\n\\[\n4^3 = 64\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 64 < 1000 \\), \\( k = 4 \\) is also a valid solution, but we need to check that \\( x = 3 \\times 4 = 12 \\) gives the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) under the given condition. If we try \\( k = 5 \\):\n\n\\[\n5^3 = 125\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 125 < 1000 \\), but \\( k = 6 \\) gives:\n\n\\[\n6^3 = 216\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 216 < 1000 \\) but \\( k = 7 \\) gives:\n\n\\[\n7^3 = 343\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 343 < 1000 \\) but \\( k = 8 \\) gives:\n\n\\[\n8^3 = 512\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 512 < 1000 \\) but \\( k = 9 \\) gives:\n\n\\[\n9^3 = 729\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 729 < 1000 \\) but \\( k = 10 \\) gives:\n\n\\[\n10^3 = 1000\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 1000 = 1000 \\), \\( k = 9 \\) is the greatest value that satisfies \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) for \\( x \\) being a multiple of 3. Therefore, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]",
