      "text": "To solve for the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) which is a positive multiple of 3 and satisfies \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we can proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Calculate the cube root of 1000:**\n   We need to determine the closest integer to the cube root of 1000. This can be done by calculating:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[3]{1000} = 10\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify the multiples of 3 that are less than 10:**\n   We list the multiples of 3 that are less than 10:\n   \\[\n   3, 6, 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the cube of each multiple of 3:**\n   To find the largest possible \\( x \\) that is a multiple of 3, we calculate using the cubes of the identified multiples:\n   \\[\n   3^3 = 27 < 1000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6^3 = 216 < 1000\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   9^3 = 729 < 1000\n   \\]\n\n4. **Select the greatest value:**\n   Among these, the largest value is:\n   \\[\n   9^3 = 729\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   Therefore, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) that is a positive multiple of 3 and satisfies \\( x^3 < 1000 \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{9}\n   \\]",
